A Python based REST framework for the Gnucash accounting application

**Please note this is a very early work in progress and should not be run
against live or production data.**

This project is designed to allow other applications to easily interact with
data from Gnucash via a RESTful API.

The API is built on the existing Python bindings and uses Flask to serve
responses in JSON.

Currently accounts, transactions, splits, customers, vendors, invoices,
bills, commodities and prices can be accessed to some degree via the
framework:

Accounts:

GET request to /accounts - returns a complete list of accounts
GET request to /accounts/<guid> - returns an individual account
GET request to /accounts/<guid>/splits - returns splits for an individual
account

GET vars:
date_posted_from - optional - only splits whose transaction was posted on or
after this date (YYYY-MM-DD)
date_posted_to - optional - only splits whose transaction was posted on or
before this date (YYYY-MM-DD)
limit - optional - maximum number of splits to return (default 100, capped at
500)
offset - optional - number of splits to skip before returning results (default
0), used together with limit for pagination
include - optional - comma-separated list of related objects to embed in each
split; supported values are "transaction" and "other_split" (default
"transaction,other_split"). Pass an empty value to omit both.

The response is an object of the form:
{"splits": [...], "total": <total matching splits>, "limit": <limit>,
"offset": <offset>}

Splits are sorted by transaction posted date (oldest first) so that paging with
limit/offset is consistent. The account embedded in each split is a shallow
representation (guid, name, type_id, description, currency) - it does not
include subaccounts or balances; use GET /accounts/<guid> for those.

Each split includes its reconciliation status:
reconcile_state - the raw single-char flag ("n" not reconciled, "c" cleared,
"y" reconciled, "f" frozen, "v" void)
reconcile_state_label - a human-readable form of reconcile_state
reconcile_date - the date the split was reconciled or cleared (YYYY-MM-DD), or
null when it is neither

GET request to /accounts/<guid>/splits/search - searches splits in an account
(and optionally its subaccounts) by description, posted date and amount

GET vars:
description - optional - case-insensitive substring match against the parent
transaction's description; empty or absent applies no constraint
date_posted_from - optional - only splits whose transaction was posted on or
after this date (YYYY-MM-DD)
date_posted_to - optional - only splits whose transaction was posted on or
before this date (YYYY-MM-DD)
amount_from - optional - minimum split amount by absolute value, so the sign
is ignored (both debits and credits of that magnitude match)
amount_to - optional - maximum split amount by absolute value, so the sign is
ignored
include_children - optional - set to "true" (or "1"/"yes") to also search the
named account's subaccounts recursively; otherwise only the named account is
searched (default false)
limit - optional - maximum number of splits to return (default 100, capped at
500)
offset - optional - number of splits to skip before returning results (default
0), used together with limit for pagination
include - optional - comma-separated list of related objects to embed in each
split; supported values are "transaction" and "other_split" (default
"transaction,other_split"). The account is always embedded and cannot be
omitted; pass an empty value to embed neither transaction nor other_split.

The filters (description, date_posted_from, date_posted_to, amount_from,
amount_to) are combined with AND - a split must satisfy every filter present to
be returned. The response, the split shape, the sorting (by transaction posted
date oldest first, with the split guid as a tiebreaker for stable paging) and
the reconcile_state/reconcile_state_label/reconcile_date fields are all as
described for GET /accounts/<guid>/splits:
{"splits": [...], "total": <total matching splits>, "limit": <limit>,
"offset": <offset>}

When include_children is false every split embeds the shallow representation
of the queried account; when it is true each split instead embeds its own
account, so results from different subaccounts carry their respective accounts.

A non-existent account guid returns 404. A malformed parameter - a date_posted
value not formatted as YYYY-MM-DD, or a non-numeric amount value - returns an
HTTP 400 with an {"errors": [...]} object (error type "InvalidSearch").

POST request to /accounts - creates a new account

POST variables:
name - required - the name of the account
currency - required - a gnucash currency code e.g. GBP
account_type_id - required - the numeric account type id (e.g. 0 for BANK,
2 for ASSET, 8 for INCOME, 9 for EXPENSE - see ACCT_TYPE_* in
libgnucash/engine/Account.h)
parent_account_guid - required - the GUID of the parent account (the root
account's GUID can be obtained from /accounts)
description - optional - a description for the account
code - optional - an account code

POST request to /transactions - creates a new transaction (currently with only
two splits)

POST variables:
currency  - required - a gnucash currency code e.g. GBP
description - required - a description for the transaction
num - optional - a transaction number
date_posted - required - a date formatted as YYYY-MM-DD
splitvalue1 - required - the value of the split on splitaccount1 
splitaccount1 - required - the GUID for the 1st account on this transaction
(can be obtained from /accounts)
splitvalue2 - required - the value of the split on splitaccount2
splitaccount2 - required - the GUID for the 2nd account on this transaction

GET request to /transactions/<guid> - returns an individual transaction 
POST request to /transactions/<guid> - edits an existing transaction (accepts
the same post variables as /transactions)
DELETE request to /transactions/<guid> - deletes a transaction

GET request to /bills - returns a list of bills

GET variables:
is_paid - optional - 1 returns paid bills, 0 unpaid
is_active  - optional - 1 returns active bills, 0 inactive
date_opened_from - optional - all transactions on and after a date formatted
as YYYY-MM-DD
date_opened_to - optional - all transactions up to and before a date formatted
as YYYY-MM-DD

POST request to /bills - creates a new bill

POST variables: 
id - optional - if no id is specified the next available sequential id will be
used
vendor_id - required - the id of the vendor of this bill (can be obtained from
/vendors)
currency - required - a gnucash currency code
date_opened - required - a date formatted as YYYY-MM-DD
notes - optional - notes for this bill

GET request to /bills/<id> - returns an individual bill
POST request to /bills/<id> - edits (and optionally posts) a bill

POST variables:
vendor_id - required - the id of the vendor of this bill (can be obtained from
/vendors)
currency - required - a gnucash currency code
date_opened - a date formatted as YYYY-MM-DD
notes - optional -  notes for this invoice
posted - optional - set to 1 if marking the invoice as posted (0 or empty
otherwise)
posted_account_guid - required if posted = 1 - the GUID for the posted account
posted_date - required if posted = 1 - a date formatted as YYYY-MM-DD
due_date - required if posted = 1 - a date formatted as YYYY-MM-DD
posted_memo - optional - a description
posted_accumulatesplits - optional - whether to accumulate splits or not (1 for
yes, 0 for no)
posted_autopay - optional - whether to auto pay or not (1 for yes, 0 for no)

PAY request to /bills/<id> - mark a bill as paid

POST variables:

posted_account_guid - required - the GUID for the posted account
transfer_account_guid - required - the GUID for the transfer account
payment_date - required - a date formatted as YYYY-MM-DD
num - optional - a number for this payment
memo - optional - a description for this payment
auto_pay - required - whether to auto pay or not (1 for yes, 0 for no)

GET request to /bills/<id>/entries  - return all entries for an individual bill
POST request to /bills/<id>/entries - adds a new entry to a bill

date - required -  a date formatted as YYYY-MM-DD
description  - required -   the description for this entry
account_guid  - required -  the GUID for the required accoumt
quantity - required - the quantity
price - required -  the price

GET request to /invoices - returns all invoices (accepts the same get variables
as /bills)
POST request to /invoices - creates a new invoice

POST variables:
id - optional - if no id is specified the next available sequential id will be
used
customer_id - required - the id of the customer of this invoice (can be
obtained from /customers)
currency - required - a gnucash currency code
date_opened - a date formatted as YYYY-MM-DD
notes - optional -  notes for this invoice

GET request to /invoices/<id> - returns an individual invoice
POST request to /invoices/<id> - edits (and optionally posts) an invoice

POST variables:
customer_id - required - the id of the customer of this bill (can be obtained
from /customers)
currency - required - a gnucash currency code
date_opened - a date formatted as YYYY-MM-DD
notes - optional -  notes for this invoice
posted - optional - set to 1 if marking the invoice as posted (0 or empty
otherwise)
posted_account_guid - required if posted = 1 - the GUID for the posted account
posted_date - required if posted = 1 - a date formatted as YYYY-MM-DD
due_date - required if posted = 1 - a date formatted as YYYY-MM-DD
posted_memo - optional - a description
posted_accumulatesplits - optional - whether to accumulate splits or not (1 for
yes, 0 for no)
posted_autopay - optional - whether to auto pay or not (1 for yes, 0 for no)

PAY request to /invoices/<id> - mark an invoice as paid (accepts the same post
variables as /bills/<id>)

GET request to /invoices/<id>/entries - return all entries for an individual
invoice
POST request to /invoices/<id>/entries - adds a new entry to an  invoice
(accepts the same post variables as /bills/<id>/entries)

GET request to /entries/<guid> - returns an individual entry 
POST request to /entries/<guid> - edits an existing entry (accepts the same
post variables as /bills/<id>/entries)

GET request to /customers - returns a list of customers
POST request to /customers - creates a new customer

POST variables:
id - optional - if no id is specified the next available sequential id will be
used
currency - required - a gnucash currency code
name - required - the customer's name
contact - optional - a contact for this customer
address_line_1 - required - the first line of the customer's address
address_line_2 - optional - the second line of the customer's address
address_line_3 - optional - the third line of the customer's address
address_line_4 - optional - the fourth line of the customer's address
phone - optional - the customer's phone number
fax - optional - the customer's fax number
email - optional - the customer's email address

GET request to /customers/<id> - returns an individual customer
POST request to /customers/<id> - edits an existing customer (accepts the same
post variables as /customers)
GET request to /customers/<id>/invoices - returns all invoices for a single
customer

GET request to /vendors - returns a list of vendors
POST request to /vendors - creates a new vendor

POST variables:
id - optional - if no id is specified the next available sequential id will be
used
currency - required - a gnucash currency code
name - required - the vendor's name
contact - optional - a contact for this vendor
address_line_1 - required - the first line of the vendor's address
address_line_2 - optional - the second line of the vendor's address
address_line_3 - optional - the third line of the vendor's address
address_line_4 - optional - the fourth line of the vendor's address
phone - optional - the vendor's phone number
fax - optional - the vendor's fax number
email - optional - the vendor's email address

GET request to /vendors/<id> - returns an individual vendor
GET request to /vendors/<id>/bills - returns all bills for a single vendor

GET request to /commodities - returns a list of commodities

GET variables:
namespace - optional - restrict to a single namespace (e.g. CURRENCY, NASDAQ)

POST request to /commodities - creates a new (non-currency) commodity, e.g.
a stock. ISO currencies are pre-populated and should be looked up rather than
created.

POST variables:
namespace - required - the namespace for this commodity (e.g. NASDAQ, AMEX)
mnemonic - required - the ticker symbol or short code (e.g. AAPL)
fullname - required - the human-readable name (e.g. "Apple Inc.")
cusip - optional - the CUSIP or ISIN identifier
fraction - optional - the smallest tradable fraction as an integer
(default 10000)
quote_source - optional - the Finance::Quote source internal name
(e.g. "yahoo_json")
quote_tz - optional - the timezone for quote retrieval

GET request to /commodities/<namespace>/<mnemonic> - returns an individual
commodity

GET request to /commodities/<namespace>/<mnemonic>/prices/latest - returns
the most recent price for the commodity against a specific currency

GET variables:
currency_mnemonic - required - the ISO currency code for the quote currency

GET request to /commodities/<namespace>/<mnemonic>/prices/nearest - returns
the price closest in time to a given date for the commodity

GET variables:
currency_mnemonic - required - the ISO currency code for the quote currency
date - required - a date formatted as YYYY-MM-DD or YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS

GET request to /prices - returns a list of prices

GET variables:
commodity_namespace - optional - restrict to a single commodity namespace
commodity_mnemonic - optional - restrict to a single commodity mnemonic
(must be combined with commodity_namespace)
currency_mnemonic - optional - restrict to a single quote currency
date_from - optional - return prices on and after a date formatted as
YYYY-MM-DD
date_to - optional - return prices up to and before a date formatted as
YYYY-MM-DD

POST request to /prices - creates a new price quote

POST variables:
commodity_namespace - required - the namespace of the priced commodity
commodity_mnemonic - required - the mnemonic of the priced commodity
currency_mnemonic - required - the ISO currency code the price is denominated
in
value - required (unless value_num + value_denom are supplied) - the price as
a decimal string (e.g. 185.42)
value_num - optional - the numerator of the price as an exact rational, must
be paired with value_denom
value_denom - optional - the denominator of the price as an exact rational
date - required - a date formatted as YYYY-MM-DD or YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS
source - optional - one of the canonical price source strings: "user:price"
(default), "user:price-editor", "Finance::Quote", "user:xfer-dialog",
"user:split-register", "user:split-import", "user:stock-split",
"user:stock-transaction", "user:invoice-post"
type - optional - one of "last", "bid", "ask", "nav", "unknown" (default
"last")

GET request to /prices/<guid> - returns an individual price
DELETE request to /prices/<guid> - removes a price from the price database

POST request to /save - flushes all in-memory changes to the backend. Changes
made through the API are otherwise only persisted when the server shuts down
cleanly. Returns 204 on success.

POST request to /revert - discards all unsaved in-memory changes and re-reads
the book from the backend. This ends and re-opens the session, so any changes
made since the server started (or since the last /save) are lost. Returns 204
on success. If re-opening the book fails the server is left with no active
session and returns 503; all other routes then return 503 until a subsequent
POST /revert succeeds in re-opening the book.

GET request to /session - reports whether the book has unsaved in-memory
changes not yet flushed to the backend. The response is an object of the form
{"dirty": <bool>}, where true means there are changes that a POST /save would
persist (and a POST /revert would discard). Returns 200 on success.

Usage
-----

**As this is work in progress you'll need a knowledge of building Gnucash from
source and experience with python.**

Full details to be provided at a later date, but in short you'll need a copy of
Gnucash built with Python bindings and the Flask module installed.

Rrun `python ./gnucash_rest <connection string>` to start the server on
<http://localhost:5000> e.g
`python ./gnucash_rest mysql://user:password@127.0.0.1/gnucash_test`

Navigate to <http://localhost:5000/invoices> and you should get your invoices
in JSON format.

The server shares a single GnuCash session across all requests and the
underlying engine is not thread-safe, so it runs single-threaded (one request
at a time) on purpose. Do not run it under a multi-worker/multi-threaded WSGI
server (e.g. gunicorn with several workers), as the workers would race on the
shared book and fight over the backend lock.

Files
-----

`gnucash_rest.py` - The Flask app which responds to REST requests with JSON
responses

`gnucash_simple.py` - A helper file to convert Gnucash objects into
dictionaries for easier conversion to JSON.

Future
------

I'm using the API already to integrate Gnucash invoices with other systems, so
I'll be adding features as and when I need them. The format of the API is
likely to change as development continues.
